before:
```swift
final class LabelOwner {
    let text: String
    init(text: String) { self.text = text }
    func makeReader() -> () -> String {
        return { [weak self] in self?.text ?? "Released" }
    }
}
```
The returned closure can outlive the owner without owning it.

After that class definition:
```swift
func releasedReader() -> () -> String {
    let owner = LabelOwner(text: "Map")
    return owner.makeReader()
}
let read = releasedReader()
print(read())
```
This prints Released. makeReader takes no explicit arguments and returns a zero-argument closure. The small releasedReader helper returns that closure after its local owner loses its last strong reference. Calling read then uses the fallback instead of accessing a released owner. No force unwrap is needed.

after:
```swift
final class LabelOwner {
    let text: String
    init(text: String) { self.text = text }
    func makeReader() -> () -> String {
        return { [weak self] in self?.text ?? "Released" }
    }
}
func releasedReader() -> () -> String {
    let owner = LabelOwner(text: "Map")
    return owner.makeReader()
}
let read = releasedReader()
print(read())
```
This prints Released. The returned closure remains available, but the local owner has no strong reference keeping it alive.
